## Proof

Let $\mathbb{R}$ be regarded as a vector space over the field $\mathbb{Q}$. A basis $B$ of this space (a Hamel basis) exists by the Axiom of Choice (Zorn's lemma). We determine $|B|$.

**Upper bound.** Since $B \subseteq \mathbb{R}$, we have $|B| \le |\mathbb{R}| = 2^{\aleph_0}$.

**Lower bound (the basis is uncountable).** Suppose for contradiction that $B$ is countable. Every $x \in \mathbb{R}$ is a *finite* linear combination $x = q_1 b_1 + \cdots + q_n b_n$ with $q_i \in \mathbb{Q}$, $b_i \in B$. The set of all such finite linear combinations is
$$\bigcup_{n=0}^{\infty}\, \mathbb{Q}^n \times B^n,$$
a countable union of countable sets (each $\mathbb{Q}^n \times B^n$ is countable when $B$ is countable). Hence it is countable. But this set equals $\mathbb{R}$, which is uncountable — contradiction. Therefore $B$ is uncountable, in particular infinite.

**Equality.** For an infinite basis $B$, the set of finite linear combinations has cardinality
$$\left|\bigcup_{n=0}^{\infty}\, \mathbb{Q}^n \times B^n\right| = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \aleph_0 \cdot |B|^n.$$
For $n \ge 1$ and infinite $|B|$, we have $|B|^n = |B|$, so each summand equals $\max(\aleph_0, |B|) = |B|$. The countable sum is therefore $\aleph_0 \cdot |B| = |B|$. Since this set is exactly $\mathbb{R}$,
$$|\mathbb{R}| = |B|.$$

Combining with the upper bound, $|B| = |\mathbb{R}| = 2^{\aleph_0}$.

**Conclusion.** The cardinality of a basis for $\mathbb{R}$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ is $\boxed{2^{\aleph_0}}$, the cardinality of the continuum.

